    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d607-a2">

                <pb facs="0680" n="600" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg600"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2—translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Jérémie to Mr. Powell</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ministry for Foreign Affairs</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Port
                                au Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-05-27"><hi rendition="#italic">May 27,
                        1902</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Minister</hi>: I have the honor to bring to your
                    knowledge that the central committee of public safety has separated after having
                    decreed the formation of a Provisional Government at Port au Prince. To me has
                    devolved the delicate mission of directing the departments of foreign relations
                    and of public instructions.</p>
                <p>Soon the people will freely choose their delegates and the National Assembly will
                    elect to the first magistracy of State a citizen that it judges worthy of its
                    confidence.</p>
                <p>Extraordinary events have agitated the country in its interior, but nothing has
                    changed its exterior politics. It aims at progress. It understands that the
                    sympathy of friendly powers will assure its evolution. This sympathy it believes
                    it merits by the good spirit that guides it, and by the protection that it gives
                    to all who dwell in its territory, both to their interests and to their
                    persons.</p>
                <p>During its short control of affairs the Provisional Government will apply itself
                    to maintain order and strengthen the bonds of friendship that attaches it to the
                    nation of which you are the worthy representative here.</p>
                <p>I venture to count, Mr. Minister, on your kindly assistance to render easy the
                    task that has been assigned to me.</p>
                <p>Please accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jérémie.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
